1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,003
Hello, world. Welcome back to
the Four Eyes Report. Today:

2
00:00:05,003 --> 00:00:07,952
how Koreans drink, what they snack on,

3
00:00:07,952 --> 00:00:10,980
and what's changing in Korea right now.

4
00:00:11,330 --> 00:00:13,010
I'm only here for the snacks!

5
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:15,280
Then let's start with the drinks.

6
00:00:15,630 --> 00:00:19,083
Let's talk about drinking.
By some statistics,

7
00:00:19,083 --> 00:00:24,037
Korea once ranked near the top of
the world in alcohol per person.

8
00:00:24,037 --> 00:00:27,790
Numbers differ by source,
but Koreans drank a LOT.

9
00:00:28,140 --> 00:00:31,970
I believe it! Korean restaurants
never turn their lights off.

10
00:00:31,970 --> 00:00:33,100
Twenty-four hours!

11
00:00:33,450 --> 00:00:36,384
But that's changing. In OECD data,

12
00:00:36,384 --> 00:00:40,268
Korea has dropped to
twenty-first as of 2021,

13
00:00:40,268 --> 00:00:44,410
and soju output has fallen
three years in a row.

14
00:00:44,760 --> 00:00:49,560
Wait, soju is down?! Soju is
SO hot overseas right now!

15
00:00:49,910 --> 00:00:53,434
Koreans still drink. Just not like before.

16
00:00:53,434 --> 00:00:56,790
It went from a marathon to a brisk walk.

17
00:00:57,140 --> 00:01:00,821
And the taste changed too.
First everyone loved wine,

18
00:01:00,821 --> 00:01:03,460
and now whisky is the cool one, right?

19
00:01:03,810 --> 00:01:08,799
Yes. "A bottle of soju for the
lady at the next table!" is out.

20
00:01:08,799 --> 00:01:11,570
"A glass of whisky" has more style.

21
00:01:11,920 --> 00:01:13,760
Smooth. Very smooth.

22
00:01:14,110 --> 00:01:19,829
The biggest change is the honsul bar.
Koreans used to drink in big groups.

23
00:01:19,829 --> 00:01:23,230
Now younger people enjoy
a good drink alone.

24
00:01:23,580 --> 00:01:26,269
But everyone there is single, and hot,

25
00:01:26,269 --> 00:01:29,100
so it turns into a place to meet people!

26
00:01:29,450 --> 00:01:34,250
You sit alone, you use a nickname,
and you talk only if you want to.

27
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:37,160
Okay, next time I'm in Korea, I'm going!

28
00:01:37,510 --> 00:01:42,219
One warning. There's no age
limit. But Korea has "ip-ppaen":

29
00:01:42,219 --> 00:01:46,221
getting turned away at the
door. Officially, never.

30
00:01:46,221 --> 00:01:49,910
In a bar full of young
people, you can feel it.

31
00:01:50,260 --> 00:01:50,900
Feel what?

32
00:01:51,250 --> 00:01:55,250
Every head turning to say,
"Sir... are you lost?"

33
00:01:55,600 --> 00:01:58,400
Don't worry, Master K. I'll
vouch for you at the door.

34
00:01:58,750 --> 00:02:01,230
Thank you. I'll just drink at home.

35
00:02:02,680 --> 00:02:06,308
Did somebody say "home"? I brought
snacks. Do you know bunsik?

36
00:02:06,308 --> 00:02:08,649
Korean snack food. And here's the thing:

37
00:02:08,649 --> 00:02:11,400
Koreans are shocked that
it's popular overseas.

38
00:02:11,750 --> 00:02:14,856
Really? It's delicious!
Of course it's popular.

39
00:02:14,856 --> 00:02:17,830
I don't understand why
they don't understand!

40
00:02:18,180 --> 00:02:22,718
Because bunsik is classic street
food. Spicy, salty, sweet,

41
00:02:22,718 --> 00:02:26,180
and you can find it
almost anywhere in Korea.

42
00:02:26,530 --> 00:02:29,970
Wait. Almost anywhere? Like, every corner?

43
00:02:30,320 --> 00:02:32,960
Every corner. And it's cheap.

44
00:02:33,310 --> 00:02:34,937
Mostly students. After school,

45
00:02:34,937 --> 00:02:38,190
they pool their allowance and
share one plate of tteokbokki.

46
00:02:38,540 --> 00:02:43,260
So it's a group project. And everyone
actually does their part: eating.

47
00:02:43,610 --> 00:02:47,014
So Koreans see a corner-stand
snack become a global hit,

48
00:02:47,014 --> 00:02:48,351
and they're surprised.

49
00:02:48,351 --> 00:02:52,970
It's like Americans finding out the
whole world loves a gas station hot dog.

50
00:02:53,320 --> 00:02:56,936
I bet it's the K-dramas.
Everyone watches them overseas,

51
00:02:56,936 --> 00:02:58,680
and the food looks amazing.

52
00:02:59,030 --> 00:03:02,148
Part of it, yes. Someone
slurps ramen in a drama,

53
00:03:02,148 --> 00:03:04,630
and a thousand people run to buy ramen.

54
00:03:04,980 --> 00:03:08,180
K-drama is an advertisement
money can't buy.

55
00:03:08,530 --> 00:03:10,696
Culture spreads, more people touch it,

56
00:03:10,696 --> 00:03:13,090
and then everyone chooses their own taste.

57
00:03:13,440 --> 00:03:17,920
Mine is tteokbokki. Spicy,
chewy, and I can't stop.

58
00:03:18,270 --> 00:03:20,900
Careful. Last time we ate
spicy, one of us died.

59
00:03:21,250 --> 00:03:23,010
And we never found out who!

60
00:03:23,360 --> 00:03:24,240
...Moving on.

61
00:03:24,590 --> 00:03:28,190
One last question. Do bunsik
shops have ip-ppaen too?

62
00:03:28,540 --> 00:03:29,170
Seriously?

63
00:03:29,520 --> 00:03:35,360
The only face control at a bunsik shop is the face
you make when someone takes the last fish cake.

64
00:03:35,710 --> 00:03:36,890
I remember that face.

65
00:03:37,240 --> 00:03:38,600
...Sorry about the fish cake.

66
00:03:38,950 --> 00:03:45,510
Enough food. Let's talk about how young
Koreans are feeling. Spoiler: bored.

67
00:03:46,960 --> 00:03:53,120
Korea has a word for the moment a hobby
suddenly stops being fun. "Taegi."

68
00:03:53,470 --> 00:03:58,750
A word for that? Like, "I used to
love running, now I hate my shoes"?

69
00:03:59,100 --> 00:04:03,057
Exactly. Golf-taegi.
Running-taegi. Reading-taegi.

70
00:04:03,057 --> 00:04:06,380
Add it to anything that stopped being fun.

71
00:04:06,730 --> 00:04:07,690
Cooking-taegi.

72
00:04:08,040 --> 00:04:11,000
Chef! You can't have cooking-taegi!

73
00:04:11,350 --> 00:04:13,660
Only for one lunch. I'm better now.

74
00:04:14,010 --> 00:04:20,588
A Seoul trend forecaster even named "the
age of boredom" the first trend of 2027.

75
00:04:20,588 --> 00:04:24,730
Too much stimulation, so
nothing feels interesting.

76
00:04:25,080 --> 00:04:27,480
So what do bored Koreans do?

77
00:04:27,830 --> 00:04:32,231
They squeeze things.
"Mallang-i," soft squishy toys.

78
00:04:32,231 --> 00:04:38,070
One type jumped seven hundred
seventy-four percent in a single month.

79
00:04:38,420 --> 00:04:40,580
Seven hundred seventy-four percent?!

80
00:04:40,930 --> 00:04:45,737
And wax-crushing balls. You crush
the hard shell with your hand.

81
00:04:45,737 --> 00:04:48,290
Sales jumped five hundred percent.

82
00:04:48,640 --> 00:04:51,280
So it's cracking an egg without the egg.

83
00:04:51,630 --> 00:04:54,953
About half of the people surveyed,
aged thirteen to fifty-nine,

84
00:04:54,953 --> 00:04:56,430
had tried something like it.

85
00:04:56,780 --> 00:05:00,860
I tried one at the airport.
It's squishy. It's very squishy.

86
00:05:01,210 --> 00:05:02,650
That's your review?

87
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:04,120
Five stars.

88
00:05:04,470 --> 00:05:06,390
And I heard about lucky turtles!

89
00:05:06,740 --> 00:05:09,854
Tiny turtles, smaller than a fingernail.

90
00:05:09,854 --> 00:05:15,460
Young Koreans carry them for luck in
love, school, work, even investing.

91
00:05:15,810 --> 00:05:18,530
Investing?! My portfolio needs a turtle.

92
00:05:18,880 --> 00:05:23,280
Slow and steady. Honestly,
that's good financial advice.

93
00:05:23,630 --> 00:05:24,750
Is the turtle edible?

94
00:05:25,100 --> 00:05:26,780
No. Chef.

95
00:05:27,130 --> 00:05:31,210
Speaking of turtles: I heard even
running is slowing down in Korea.

96
00:05:31,560 --> 00:05:31,944
True.

97
00:05:31,944 --> 00:05:37,480
Seoul held a run called "My Pace
Morning." You finish at your own speed.

98
00:05:37,830 --> 00:05:40,150
A race where nobody loses?

99
00:05:40,500 --> 00:05:42,922
Nobody wins, nobody checks the clock.

100
00:05:42,922 --> 00:05:45,540
Maybe that's the cure for running-taegi.

101
00:05:45,890 --> 00:05:48,610
I'll be at the finish line. With snacks.

102
00:05:50,060 --> 00:05:53,437
One more change. Chuseok.
Korean Thanksgiving,

103
00:05:53,437 --> 00:05:56,300
the biggest family holiday of the year.

104
00:05:56,650 --> 00:06:01,164
Everyone goes home, eats a mountain
of food, and does the ancestral rites,

105
00:06:01,164 --> 00:06:01,530
right?

106
00:06:01,880 --> 00:06:03,792
Less and less. In 2016,

107
00:06:03,792 --> 00:06:09,197
about seventy-four percent of
households planned ancestral rites.

108
00:06:09,197 --> 00:06:12,440
As of 2026, about twenty-seven percent.

109
00:06:12,790 --> 00:06:15,270
From three-quarters to one-quarter!

110
00:06:15,620 --> 00:06:20,578
And forty-three percent planned
only a family gathering. No rites,

111
00:06:20,578 --> 00:06:21,780
no grave visits.

112
00:06:22,130 --> 00:06:26,217
Some families don't even go
home. They book a hotel in Seoul.

113
00:06:26,217 --> 00:06:27,490
A "hotel vacation."

114
00:06:27,840 --> 00:06:31,520
Because the stress isn't the
food. It's the questions.

115
00:06:31,870 --> 00:06:35,950
"When are you getting married? Do you
have a job? How much do you make?"

116
00:06:36,300 --> 00:06:40,060
Three questions. Same
relatives. Every year.

117
00:06:40,410 --> 00:06:45,000
I have a strategy. When an aunt asks,
"When are you getting married?" I say,

118
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:46,570
"Have you tried the jeon?"

119
00:06:46,920 --> 00:06:53,160
Jeon. Pan-fried treats, a
Chuseok classic. A secret weapon.

120
00:06:53,510 --> 00:06:56,790
Works every time. A full
mouth asks no questions.

121
00:06:57,140 --> 00:07:00,180
Then the cousin comes up. "Your
cousin just got promoted."

122
00:07:00,530 --> 00:07:03,730
There is always a cousin. Always perfect.

123
00:07:04,080 --> 00:07:08,640
I'm a doctor! And I still hear,
"But have you met someone nice?"

124
00:07:08,990 --> 00:07:12,190
Doctor, even doctors lose to relatives.

125
00:07:12,540 --> 00:07:14,060
I just bring more jeon.

126
00:07:14,410 --> 00:07:18,490
So the new Chuseok is a
hotel, a movie, and silence?

127
00:07:18,840 --> 00:07:23,552
Not exactly. Most people still
gather. The ritual is changing.

128
00:07:23,552 --> 00:07:24,920
The reunion stays.

129
00:07:25,270 --> 00:07:28,070
And the food? Please tell
me the food survived.

130
00:07:28,420 --> 00:07:33,540
It did. Songpyeon, half-moon rice
cakes steamed on pine needles,

131
00:07:33,540 --> 00:07:35,780
are still a Chuseok classic.

132
00:07:36,130 --> 00:07:38,077
Finally, my department. Pine needles.

133
00:07:38,077 --> 00:07:40,130
The whole kitchen smells like a forest.

134
00:07:40,480 --> 00:07:42,560
Even Chuseok has a skip button now.

135
00:07:42,910 --> 00:07:45,390
Just don't skip the call to Grandma.

136
00:07:45,740 --> 00:07:51,856
So here's my take. Korea changes fast.
But people still want the same thing:

137
00:07:51,856 --> 00:07:56,684
someone to share a plate with,
a drink with, a holiday with.

138
00:07:56,684 --> 00:07:59,420
Even if the holiday is in a hotel.

139
00:07:59,770 --> 00:08:03,290
Tell us in the comments: which
Korean habit confuses you most?

140
00:08:03,640 --> 00:08:08,096
Subscribe, and we'll see you
next Friday. Stay curious.

141
00:08:08,096 --> 00:08:09,960
Keep your lenses clean.
